    "created_at": "2024-07-24T08:51:49Z",
    "updated_at": "2024-07-24T08:51:49Z",
    "body": "> Hmm, why not use the \"next timer ID\" solution discussed in that issue?\r\n\r\nStandardising how browsers select timer IDs to the wrapping algorithm is probably a good idea, but that issue is actually orthogonal to the bug I'm trying to fix here.\r\n\r\nThe problem is that clearTimeout() doesn't actually stop the \"Run in Parallel\" steps that are going to eventually run the callback, it just removes the timer ID from the map of timers. So *any* scheme that can reuse timer IDs can run into the bug, including the one you're proposing.\r\nImagine a situation where a really long timer is created with `setTimeout()`, then cleared with `clearTimeout()`, and then many other timers are made with `setTimeout()` so that the timer ID eventually wraps around. Then that original long setTimeout callback would be run because it's ID was reused. Not a very realistic situation, but still a spec bug.\r\n\r\nHonestly, I may be wringing my hands about implementing the logic in the \"specification language\" too much. Maybe just some prose describing how clearTimeout cancels the \"Run in parallel\" steps would be better. I feel I could do with some guidance, what are people's thoughts on that?",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
