    "updated_at": "2024-07-29T07:28:49Z",
    "body": "This took me a while to wrap my head around, but I think I see.\r\n\r\nI am hesitant to leave tombstone in the timers map, since that causes us to further diverge from browser's timer ID allocation algorithms. So I explored how to do as you suggest, and abort the in parallel step.\r\n\r\nOne way to do this, is to change step 5 of [run steps after a timeout](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#run-steps-after-a-timeout) to [abort when](https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/) _global_'s map of active timers[timerKey] \"is removed\".\r\n\r\nThis suffers from being bad spec-writing because it's [\"comefrom\" style](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COMEFROM). What I mean by \"is removed\" is not a rigorous concept, and refers to \"someone somewhere else in the spec performed a call to [remove](https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#list-remove)\". (In particular, it does _not_ mean \"the entry does not [exist](https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#map-exists) in the map\". It might currently exist, but have been removed and re-added sometime during the waiting step!)\r\n\r\nInverting this so that it becomes rigorous is somewhat painful, indeed. You need some way of going from the removal operation to the in-parallel steps.\r\n\r\nThe fundamental problem here is that we're using the map of active timers both to represent the developer-facing timer IDs, and to represent the \"OS job scheduler\". The former suffers from the finitide of `long`; the latter is, at least in spec abstraction land, infinite. (And in practice is probably at least 64 bits, instead of 31.) This feels somewhat related to how non-setTimeout/setInterval users of \"run step after a timeout\" end up using \"unique non-numeric values\".\r\n\r\nThus I think the best approach here would be to decouple the two. Something like:\r\n\r\n- Make \"map of active timers\" always keyed by [unique internal values](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#unique-internal-value).\r\n- Add an additional \"map of setTimeout/setInterval handles\" which maps from `long`s to \"map of active timer\" keys.\r\n- setTimeout() creates an entry in \"map of setTimeout/setInterval handles\" pointing to a newly-created entry in the \"map of active timers\". The latter entry represents the in-parallel steps.\r\n- The in parallel steps can \"abort when\" the \"map of active timers\" entry [does not exist](https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#map-exists) for the appropriate key.\r\n- clearTimeout() clears both from \"map of setTimeout/setInterval handles\" (thus freeing up that handle for future calls) and from \"map of active timers\" (thus aborting the scheduled in-parallel steps)\r\n- This should let us get rid of the inelegant argument _timerKey_ to \"run steps after a timeout\". I think it returns the unique key inside \"map of active timers\", so that callers like setTimeout() can use it for future cancelation.\r\n\r\nDoes this make sense? Are you interested in tackling it? It's totally OK to say no, as I realize this ballooned a good bit beyond what I thought was an easy first-time contribution :)",
